  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday morning through next Monday)\nIssued at 211 PM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nWe will get our first taste of fall to kick off the long term period \n(Thursday) across North and Central Georgia. A strengthening low \npressure system traveling northward just off the Mid-Atlantic coast \nline will help to reinforce a strong high pressure (1035+mb) in \nplace over the far NE US and Southern Canada. There may be just \nenough moisture to support isolated rain showers at the higher \nelevations on Thursday but most places will remain dry. Drier air \nfiltering into the region by way of north to northeasterly low/mid-\nlevel flow will result in more seasonal temperatures and near-zero \nrain chances through the weekend. Forecast highs will be in the 70s \nto low 80s (60s in the mountains) and forecast lows in the 50s/60s \nThursday through Friday. A slight warm up is likely to occur over \nthe weekend into early next week as daytime temperatures return to \nthe mid- to upper 80s. However, North and Central Georgia's \nplacement, on the western side of the departing low pressure system \noff the Atlantic and weak midlevel ridging to our west should keep \ndrier air in the area lending to continued comfortable temperatures \ndespite the slight 'warm up.'",
